de what we're  gonna do is first we're gonna define  this randomized SVD  function okay so basically what you  would normally use the SVD function for  now you can use this randomized SVD and  then we're going to apply it to a high  resolution image to make sure that it  doesn't mess anything up okay so there's  a math version of this lecture the  previous lecture was just kind of the  math of the randomized SVD here's the  code  and so you'll remind yourself that this  randomized SVD on a matrix X you need to  know what your target rank R is how many  columns of U and V and Sigma you want to  pull out  that's our Q is what we call the the  number of power iterations I'll talk  about that in a minute and P is the over  sampling factor so for now you can kind  of forget about Q and P I'll talk about  those in a minute but the basic idea of  the randomized SVD is that you take your  original data matrix X let's let's draw  this actually pictorially your original  data matrix X and you multiply it by a  random projection matrix P so that what  you get out is kind of a tall skinny  matrix Z and this random projection  matrix P could be could have our columns  where R is the target rank that's kind  of the simple version of the story and  with high probability Z will have the  same kind of dominant column SVD as X  it'll have some of the same features in  the column space it'll it'll represent  the column space of X they'll have  similar spans now this over sampling  factor P oftentimes we find that it's  capital P often we find that it's useful  to sample a few more columns of X P more  columns where P is a small number 5 or  10 and we get a better sketch of the  column space of X so that's over  sampling and then what we do is we  compute the QR factorization of Z to get  an orthogonal basis an orthonormal basis  for this column space  that's this QR here very simple then  what we do is we take X and we project  it into that QR that  Subspace to get this Y matrix which is  very very small rank R or R plus P and  then we do the SVD of Y of this Y matrix  and we use that SVD to estimate the SVD  of X to get the U the Sigma and the V of  the original X matrix by computing the  SVD of a really really really small  matrix Y okay that's the basic idea  power iterations would essentially mean  that if if the singular value  distribution of X doesn't decay fast  enough maybe we multiply X by x times X  transpose a few times and that'll make  the singular values look look like they  drop off faster I'll tell you about that  in a minute  but that's what this little code here in  the middle is doing for power iterations  long story short you know SVD is a  one-line Python command randomized SVD  is just a few more lines it's like ten  lines not much more complexity but it's  going to allow us to approximate this  this rank our decomposition much much  faster okay and with very little loss in  accuracy in fact you can get tunable  error bounds as a function of Q and P so  you can select those so that you can get  a specified accuracy in your in your  model so what we're gonna do is we're  going to read in an image of the planet  Jupiter I'm gonna plot the high  resolution image of Jupiter we're going  to compute the deterministic the regular  SVD of rank 400 we're going to plot that  next to it so the rank 400 approximation  using the expensive SVD and then we're  going to compute the same rake 400  approximation using our fast efficient  randomized svv okay so the target rank  is 400 I've kind of pre-selected that I  know that that's enough rows and columns  to get a good representation of Jupiter  we're gonna do one power iteration and  have an over sampling factor of five  we're gonna have five more columns in  our sketch matrix than 400 which is not  an act not that much of an extra costs  we're going to go from a 400 column Z to  a 405 column C and then we're going to  use those SVD approximations to  reconstruct the planet Jupiter and 